---
// Custom CHS case study. Replaces the Notion render. Notion still drives
// metadata (title, tags, cover, order) for the homepage list; only the
// body content is custom.
//
// Narrative spine: it's the second person's story. YOU are the non-technical
// employee who logs in lost and leaves having done the thing. "I" is the
// designer (Jon) who made the bet and shipped the comprehension surface that
// closes the gap between what the platform can do and what you'd think to ask.

import CSLayout from "../../components/case-study/CSLayout.astro";
import CSHero from "../../components/case-study/CSHero.astro";
import CSShortVersion from "../../components/case-study/CSShortVersion.astro";
import CSSection from "../../components/case-study/CSSection.astro";
import CSPullquote from "../../components/case-study/CSPullquote.astro";
import CSStatGrid from "../../components/case-study/CSStatGrid.astro";
import ChsRolePicker from "../../components/case-study/showcase/ChsRolePicker.astro";
import ComprehensionSurface from "../../components/case-study/showcase/ComprehensionSurface.astro";

export const prerender = true;
---

<CSLayout
  title="CHS — Jonathan Tollefson"
  description="Teaching AI to 10,000 employees. The internal AI platform shipped — then the comprehension surface had to be designed. Sole UX function, embedded with engineering."
  ogImage="/case-studies/chs/cover.png"
>
  <CSHero
    eyebrow="November 2025 — present"
    title="CHS"
    subtitle="Teaching AI to 10,000 employees."
    cover="/case-studies/chs/cover.png"
    meta={[
      { label: "Role", value: "Sole UX function, embedded with engineering" },
      { label: "Timeline", value: "Nov 2025 — present (launched Mar 2026)" },
      { label: "Stack", value: "Open WebUI · Azure · Figma" },
      { label: "Status", value: "Shipped · company-wide rollout in progress" },
    ]}
  />

  <CSShortVersion rows={[
    { k: "the problem", v: "CHS — 10,000 employees — met enterprise AI, and nobody could see what the tools could actually do for their job." },
    { k: "what I did", v: "Designed and taught the comprehension layer: from a 250-person beta out across the company, with non-technical teams building their own models." },
    { k: "the outcome", v: "Zero back-channel pings to engineering; teams self-serve. Rolling company-wide since March 2026." },
  ]} />

  {/* ─────── COLD OPEN ─────── */}
  <CSSection title="You just got access" deck="A 10,000-person agricultural cooperative. The internal AI platform is live, and your name is on the list." noTopRule>
    <div class="cold-open" data-cold-open>
      <p class="cold-line" style="--i:0">The invite lands in your inbox.</p>
      <p class="cold-line" style="--i:1">You click the link. The platform opens.</p>
      <p class="cold-line" style="--i:2">It can build knowledge bases. Train custom models. Call functions. Query CHS data. All of it.</p>
      <p class="cold-line cold-line-beat" style="--i:3">You have no idea where to start.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>That blank moment — not the model — is the whole problem. You're not behind. You're in sales, or finance, or ops, and you should never need to know what an embedding is to get value from AI at work.</p>
    </div>

    {/* The reasoning beat — the BET, before the patterns are shown as its proof. */}
    <div class="the-bet" data-bet>
      <span class="the-bet-eyebrow">My bet</span>
      <p class="the-bet-line">I was the sole UX function on the platform team, embedded with engineering. When people didn't get it, the team's first reflex was <em>more docs</em>. Mine was the opposite — the problem was never how <em>much</em> we explained. It was the gap between what the platform could do and what you'd think to <em>ask</em>. Close that, and the manual stops mattering.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <CSPullquote hero>
        Documentation is product. The moment your answer to “users don't get it” is a Confluence page, you've already lost.
      </CSPullquote>
    </div>

    <CSStatGrid
      stats={[
        { n: 10000, label: "employees in the target audience", color: "white" },
        { n: 250, label: "beta cohort, pre-launch", color: "cyan" },
        { n: 3, label: "friction patterns surfaced", color: "yellow" },
        { n: 1, label: "comprehension surface to design", color: "pink" },
      ]}
    />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── SEE THE GAP — the comprehension surface ─────── */}
  <CSSection title="The comprehension surface" deck="Watch the gap open. Left, everything the platform can do. Right, what you actually came to ask. Nothing on one side answers the other.">
    <ComprehensionSurface />

    {/* the three frictions, promoted from a parenthetical to first-class lines */}
    <div class="frictions">
      <p class="frictions-lead">Diagnosed from biweekly research with the 250-person pre-launch beta — already licensed for GPT Enterprise, power users in name, a diagnostic cohort in practice. Three frictions kept surfacing, and not one of them was about the model:</p>
      <ul class="frictions-list">
        <li class="friction"><strong class="friction-name">Role disconnect</strong> — <span class="friction-say">“I can't tell what this does for <em>my</em> job.”</span></li>
        <li class="friction"><strong class="friction-name">Tool confusion</strong> — <span class="friction-say">“How is this different from the GPT license I already have?”</span></li>
        <li class="friction"><strong class="friction-name">Setup friction</strong> — <span class="friction-say">a custom knowledge base meant a back-channel ping to engineering. Self-serve in name only.</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>So the deliverable was never a wiki. Those four patterns in the bridge box aren't a slide deck — they're a surface you put your hands on, built so the docs and the platform are the same gesture. So put your hands on it.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── LIVE THE GAP CLOSED — role picker, promoted to the spine ─────── */}
  <CSSection title="It already speaks your job" deck="The sharpest friction in the beta was five words: “what's this for, for me?” So the fix meets you there." noTopRule>
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Pick your role. The assistant already speaks your language — its starters are your real jobs, not a feature menu. Tap one and watch it run, exactly as it will the morning you actually log in.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-bleed surface-stage">
      <div class="surface-stage-inner">
        <p class="surface-stage-eyebrow"><span class="surface-stage-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>the surface · pick a role, run a starter</p>
        <ChsRolePicker />
      </div>
    </div>

    <CSPullquote cite="Reflection, post-launch">
      The dogfooded docs-chat is the move I'd most repeat. You learn the mental model by inhabiting it — not by studying it.
    </CSPullquote>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── OUTCOME ─────── */}
  <CSSection title="What shipped" deck="Since the March launch, you're not the exception. Departmental users across CHS are standing up custom models on internal data — themselves, first try.">
    <div class="outcome-zero">
      <div class="outcome-zero-n">0</div>
      <div class="outcome-zero-body">
        <p class="outcome-zero-lead">back-channel pings to engineering</p>
        <p class="outcome-zero-sub">You just did, in one chat, what all 250 beta users had to file a ticket for. The setup friction that defined the beta is gone — self-serve is finally self-serve.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <CSStatGrid
      stats={[
        { n: 250, label: "beta cohort", color: "white" },
        { n: 10000, label: "company-wide reach (rolling)", color: "green" },
        { n: 4, label: "interaction patterns shipped as the surface", color: "yellow" },
      ]}
    />

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Other internal teams have started inheriting the surface as a design system for their own tools on Open WebUI. The onboarding gesture quietly became infrastructure.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── WHAT GENERALIZES — two principles, then the payoff ─────── */}
  <CSSection title="What I'd carry forward" deck="Two principles for any enterprise AI tool with a non-technical audience — and the one line they add up to." width="bleed">
    <div class="creed">
      <article class="creed-stmt" data-creed style="--i:0">
        <span class="creed-kicker">01</span>
        <p class="creed-line"><em>Documentation is product.</em></p>
        <p class="creed-sub">The docs have to live where the work happens, in the same interaction patterns as the tool. If the docs and the tool look different, the docs are already losing.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="creed-stmt" data-creed style="--i:1">
        <span class="creed-kicker">02</span>
        <p class="creed-line"><em>Teach by inhabiting.</em></p>
        <p class="creed-sub">A dogfooded docs-chat collapses “read the manual” into “try it.” Every gesture you make in the docs transfers verbatim to tomorrow's real build. The cheapest pedagogy you can buy.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* the closing image — full-bleed, brand-gradient accent, on the page canvas; the third line the creed adds up to */}
  <section class="closer" data-closer aria-labelledby="closer-heading">
    <div class="closer-inner">
      <span class="closer-kicker">03 · the line it adds up to</span>
      <h2 class="closer-line" id="closer-heading">The bottleneck <br />is rarely <br /><span class="closer-grad">the AI.</span></h2>
      <p class="closer-sub">The model is fine. The bottleneck is the comprehension surface between capability and audience — and that gap is where senior design earns its keep.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CSSection title="Coda" noTopRule width="narrow">
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The site you're reading is the same approach in miniature: shared primitives reused across pages, each case study hand-built on them. The <a href="/case-studies/the-console">Finishable case study</a> takes it further.</p>
      <p class="muted">Specifics are kept high-level out of respect for ongoing project confidentiality. Happy to walk through the rest in conversation.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  <div class="cs-foot">
    <a href="/#work" class="cs-foot-back">← back to selected work</a>
  </div>
</CSLayout>

<script>
  // Scroll-gated reveals for the cold open, the bet, the creed statements, and
  // the closing image. One observer; each target adds .in once when it crosses
  // ~25% into view. prefers-reduced-motion → reveal immediately, no gate.
  // (The CSS already forces a final visible state under reduced motion; this
  //  just makes sure .in lands too so nothing depends on timing.)
  const reduce = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;
  const targets = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(
    "[data-cold-open], [data-bet], [data-creed], [data-closer]"
  );

  if (reduce) {
    targets.forEach((t) => t.classList.add("in"));
  } else {
    const io = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries, obs) => {
        entries.forEach((e) => {
          if (e.isIntersecting) {
            e.target.classList.add("in");
            obs.unobserve(e.target);
          }
        });
      },
      { threshold: 0.25 }
    );
    targets.forEach((t) => {
      io.observe(t);
      // If it's already on screen at load (e.g. the cold open above the fold),
      // reveal right away so it never sits invisible waiting for a scroll.
      const r = t.getBoundingClientRect();
      if (r.top < window.innerHeight * 0.85 && r.bottom > 0) {
        t.classList.add("in");
        io.unobserve(t);
      }
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  .prose-block { max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose-block > .cs-pullquote { max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose-block .muted { color: var(--cs-text-muted); font-size: 13px; }

  /* ── COLD OPEN — a scene, lines fading in one at a time ── */
  .cold-open {
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin: 0 0 24px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
  }
  .cold-line {
    font-family: var(--cs-mono);
    font-size: clamp(17px, 2vw, 21px);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--cs-prose);
    margin: 0;
    max-width: none;
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(8px);
    transition: opacity 520ms ease, transform 520ms ease;
  }
  .cold-open.in .cold-line {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: none;
    transition-delay: calc(var(--i) * 420ms);
  }
  .cold-line-beat {
    color: var(--cs-accent);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(20px, 2.6vw, 26px);
    margin-top: 8px;
  }

  /* ── THE BET — the designer's reasoning beat, set off with an orange rule ── */
  .the-bet {
    max-width: 64ch;
    margin: 32px 0;
    padding: 4px 0 4px 20px;
    border-left: 2px solid rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.55);
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(12px);
    transition: opacity 600ms ease, transform 600ms ease;
  }
  .the-bet.in { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  .the-bet-eyebrow {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--cs-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.28em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--cs-accent);
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .the-bet-line {
    font-family: var(--cs-mono);
    font-size: clamp(17px, 2vw, 21px);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--cs-text);
    margin: 0;
    max-width: none;
  }
  .the-bet-line em { font-style: italic; color: var(--cs-accent); }

  /* ── THE FRICTIONS — first-class labeled lines under the gap diagram ── */
  .frictions {
    margin: 18px 0 0;
    max-width: 80ch;
  }
  .frictions-lead {
    font-family: var(--cs-mono);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--cs-text-muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    max-width: 80ch;
  }
  .frictions-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 9px;
  }
  .friction {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 16px;
    font-family: var(--cs-mono);
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--cs-text-muted);
  }
  .friction::before {
    content: "·";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--cs-accent);
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .friction-name {
    color: var(--cs-text);
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .friction-say { font-style: italic; }
  .friction-say em { color: var(--cs-text); font-style: italic; font-weight: 700; }

  /* ── THE SURFACE STAGE — full-bleed band the role picker is built into ── */
  /* Product theater: the stage keeps its dark skin in BOTH temperatures, so
     the --cs-* set is pinned here to its night values for everything inside,
     and the band's own chrome uses literals rather than the flipping tokens.
     ChsRolePicker renders only inside this stage, which is why it carries no
     day/night handling of its own. */
  .surface-stage {
    --cs-bg: #07060c;
    --cs-surface: #0f0a12;
    --cs-surface-2: #15101a;
    --cs-text: #f5f1e8;
    --cs-text-muted: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.62);
    --cs-text-dim: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.38);
    --cs-line: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.10);
    --cs-line-strong: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.22);
    --cs-prose: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.88);
    --cs-accent: var(--brand-orange);
    --cs-pink-text: var(--brand-pink);
    --cs-cyan-text: var(--brand-cyan);
    --cs-green-text: var(--brand-green);
    --cs-yellow-text: var(--brand-yellow);
    --cs-grad-text: var(--brand-grad);
    background:
      radial-gradient(80% 60% at 50% 0%, rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.06) 0%, transparent 70%),
      #050409;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.10);
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.10);
    padding: clamp(36px, 6vw, 68px) 0;
    /* longhand only — `margin:` shorthand resets .cs-bleed's margin-left:50%
       (atlas gotcha #2) and shoves this full-bleed band half a viewport left. */
    margin-top: 28px;
    margin-bottom: 28px;
  }
  .surface-stage-inner {
    max-width: 1140px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 32px;
  }
  .surface-stage-eyebrow {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    font-family: var(--cs-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--cs-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 20px;
  }
  .surface-stage-dot {
    width: 7px;
    height: 7px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--brand-green);
    box-shadow: 0 0 6px var(--brand-green);
    animation: surface-stage-pulse 1.6s ease-in-out infinite;
  }
  @keyframes surface-stage-pulse {
    0%, 100% { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1); }
    50% { opacity: 0.42; transform: scale(0.8); }
  }

  /* ── OUTCOME ZERO — the one stat pulled out of the grid and made to land ── */
  .outcome-zero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
    gap: 24px;
    align-items: center;
    margin: 0 0 32px;
    padding: 22px 26px;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--cs-cyan-text) 5%, transparent);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--cs-cyan-text) 25%, transparent);
    border-radius: 6px;
  }
  .outcome-zero-n {
    font-family: var(--cs-mono);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(40px, 5vw, 56px);
    line-height: 0.9;
    color: var(--cs-cyan-text);
    font-feature-settings: "tnum";
  }
  .outcome-zero-lead {
    font-family: var(--cs-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--cs-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    max-width: none;
  }
  .outcome-zero-sub {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--cs-prose);
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 56ch;
  }
  .outcome-zero-sub strong { color: var(--cs-text); font-weight: 700; }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .outcome-zero { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 8px; }
  }

  /* ── WHAT GENERALIZES — oversized mono-italic statements, one per breath ── */
  .creed {
    max-width: 1040px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    display: grid;
    gap: 64px;
    padding: 8px 32px 12px;
  }
  .creed-stmt {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(20px);
    transition: opacity 700ms ease, transform 700ms ease;
  }
  .creed-stmt.in { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  .creed-kicker {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--cs-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.28em;
    color: var(--cs-accent);
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }
  .creed-line {
    font-family: var(--cs-mono);
    font-size: clamp(28px, 6vw, 64px);
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--cs-text);
    margin: 0;
    max-width: none;
    overflow-wrap: break-word;
  }
  .creed-line em { font-style: italic; color: var(--cs-text); }
  .creed-sub {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--cs-text-muted);
    margin: 18px 0 0;
    max-width: 56ch;
  }

  /* ── the closing image — full-bleed prose with a brand-gradient accent.
     Pure page copy, so it rides the canvas: on day the orange bloom sits
     straight on the ground with no plate under it; night restores the dark
     plate below, value for value. ── */
  .closer {
    position: relative;
    width: 100vw;
    margin-left: 50%;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    /* hand-rolled bleed: same viewport-centering correction as .cs-bleed
       (the rail-padded parent shifts 50% by 46px — review, 2026-08-25) */
    margin-top: clamp(120px, 16vw, 200px);
    margin-bottom: clamp(72px, 10vw, 120px);
    min-height: min(82vh, 760px);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    background:
      radial-gradient(80% 70% at 50% 50%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand-orange) 7%, transparent) 0%, transparent 68%);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--cs-line);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--cs-line);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  /* screen-only, matching the layout's night block — print must never
     resolve a dark plate under day ink */
  @media screen {
    :global(html[data-night]) .closer {
      background:
        radial-gradient(80% 70% at 50% 50%, rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.10) 0%, transparent 68%),
        #060509;
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .closer {
      margin-left: calc(50% - var(--railw, 110px) / 2);
    }
  }
  .closer-inner {
    max-width: 1160px;
    width: 100%;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: clamp(64px, 12vw, 140px) 32px;
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(24px);
    transition: opacity 900ms ease, transform 900ms ease;
  }
  .closer.in .closer-inner { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  .closer-kicker {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--cs-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--cs-text-dim);
    margin-bottom: 24px;
  }
  .closer-line {
    font-family: var(--cs-mono);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(30px, 11vw, 132px);
    line-height: 0.98;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    color: var(--cs-text);
    margin: 0;
    max-width: none;
    overflow-wrap: break-word;
  }
  /* the hard breaks are a desktop composition; below 720px the fluid display
     face is already at the measure's limit, so let the line wrap naturally. */
  .closer-line br { display: none; }
  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .closer-line br { display: inline; }
  }
  .closer-grad {
    background: var(--cs-grad-text);
    -webkit-background-clip: text;
    background-clip: text;
    color: transparent;
  }
  .closer-sub {
    font-family: var(--cs-mono);
    font-size: clamp(17px, 2vw, 21px);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--cs-text-muted);
    margin: 32px 0 0;
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .cold-line,
    .the-bet,
    .creed-stmt,
    .closer-inner {
      transition: none;
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
    }
    .cold-open.in .cold-line { transition-delay: 0ms; }
    .surface-stage-dot { animation: none; }
  }
</style>
